Z||000006||Phil||04-16-2000||07:38 AM||||Yes, but only once.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||04-16-2000||09:39 AM||||The Tach Filter is basically an electrolytic capacitor to filter out any excess noise so the tach reads RPM steady. If it opens up (stops filtering), the tach will be "jumpy" if it shorts out the tach won't work. You could probably buy a capacitor at radio shack that is cheaper but you need to know what the capacitance is, and I haven't been able to find that out yet.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||04-16-2000||09:46 PM||||Technically the tach filter is a resistor-capacitor network, not a simple capacitor.<P>GM specs show it as 2 caps between the signal line and ground with a couple resistor in the line. It's job is to clean up and limit signal level to the tach. (Tho I'm told it does a fairly poor job at the clean up part.)<P>The car will run perfectly fine without the filter but the tach won't work.<P>I'm going to try to grab one this week for disection. I'll let every one know what I find. (Likely the damn thing is potted..... may not be able to disect it.)||||reg||1
Z||000009||Oreif||04-16-2000||11:07 PM||||The tach filter cannot limit the level with the circuit you describe. The filter is also not an R/C circuit. The Circuit you describe is a 2 stage bandpass filter. This is very simular to the "Noise Suppressors" you can buy for car stereos. The resistor is an Impedence matching resistor for the network. Capacitors in parallel are additive. So if cap "a" is 1 Farad and cap "b" is 1 Farad the circuit would be 2 Farads. The impedence is important so you don't create a ground loop with the armature of the tach needle. <BR>I guess 21 years of electronics pays off!||||reg||4
